iki = wikipedia_search(query=\"current pope age\")\nprint(\"Pope age as per wikipedia:\", pope_age_wiki)\npope_age_search = web_search(query=\"current pope age\")\nprint(\"Pope age as per google search:\", pope_age_search)\n</code>\nObservation:\nPope age: \"The pope Francis is currently 88 years old.\"\n\nThought: I know that the pope is 88 years old. Let's compute the result using Python code.\n<code>\npope_current_age = 88 ** 0.36\nfinal_answer(pope_current_age)\n</code>\n\nAbove examples were using notional tools that might not exist for you. On top of performing computations in the Python code snippets that you create, you only have access to these tools, behaving like regular python functions:\n<code>\ndef list_files(path: string, limit: integer, offset: integer) -> object:\n    \"\"\"List text files under a repository path.\n\n    Args:\n        path: path\n        limit: limit\n        offset: offset\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef search_code(query: string, paths: array, limit: integer) -> object:\n    \"\"\"Search visible text source files for a literal string.\n\n    Args:\n        query: query\n        paths: paths\n        limit: limit\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef read_file(path: string) -> object:\n    \"\"\"Read a text file in full.\n\n    Args:\n        path: path\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef read_range(path: string, start_line: integer, end_line: integer) -> object:\n    \"\"\"Read an inclusive line range from a text file.\n\n    Args:\n        path: path\n        start_line: start line\n        end_line: end line\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef read_symbol(path: string, symbol: string) -> object:\n    \"\"\"Read a complete Python class or function including decorators.\n\n    Args:\n        path: path\n        symbol: symbol\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef write_file(path: string, content: string, expected_hash: string) -> object:\n    \"\"\"Replace an editable text file after checking its current hash.\n\n    Args:\n        path: path\n        content: content\n        expected_hash: expected hash\n    \"